{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Send for yourself men, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says <b>for yourself</b>, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 34b): according to your knowledge — I am not commanding you, etc. And it is difficult: why would HaShem agree to the evil that they proposed to do to His people, so that His utterance would appear upon the counsel of the wicked? To understand the matter, I will note the following: from whose womb did the great evil that resulted from the spies emerge? If from the side of the deed itself, there is nothing wrong in it, for do we not find (Joshua 2:1) that Joshua likewise sent two men to spy and they spied out the land? If so, the matter is not ugly in itself. And if from the side of the men who spied, that they were wicked — do we not find that they were chosen by the word of HaShem, who tests hearts and kidneys, and the verse testifies about them: ״כֻּלָּם אֲנָשִׁים״ — that they were righteous?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the matter will be explained according to their words of blessed memory, who said (Yalkut Shimoni 782), and this is their language: At first they gathered before Moses to send the spies and he did not wish it, and he said to them: HaShem has already promised us. And they said to him: the nations know about us that we are coming to dispossess them, and they are hiding their property, and when Israel arrive they will find nothing, so the word of HaShem will be nullified, heaven forbid. And then ״וַיִּיטַב הַדָּבָר בְּעֵינֵי מֹשֶׁה״ — what they said (Deuteronomy 1:22): ״וְיַחְפְּרוּ לָנוּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״, meaning the excavations in which they hide their property, etc. When Moses heard this, he was caught in their hands — end quote. Now, from what they of blessed memory precisely stated — that he was ״caught in their hands״ — it indicates that they spoke with deception and trapped him with cunning, and it is not known what the deception was that they spoke with their mouths by which they trapped him." "<b>Indeed,</b> there are two kinds of spying. The first is spying to know the route by which they should ascend, and also to know whether they should muster all of Israel to go up or only a portion of them, as in the words of the spies of Ai who answered Joshua (Joshua 7:3): ״אַל יַעַל כָּל הָעָם, כְּאַלְפַּיִם אִישׁ אוֹ כִּשְׁלֹשֶׁת אֲלָפִים אִישׁ יַעֲלוּ וגו׳, אַל תְּיַגַּע שָׁמָּה אֶת כָּל הָעָם״. And this kind of spying only scouts the city they are heading to, and once they conquer it and decide to besiege another city, they scout them one after another. The second kind of spying is general: this indicates that they wish to see and assess whether they have the strength to conquer, and this is the spying that Israel agreed to send — for the entirety of the land of Canaan, as is explicit in the account. And this tells of a lack of faith in HaShem and a seeking of pretext. Therefore they said in the Midrash that Moses did not wish it until they schemed with deception and told him that the reason they were sending was on account of the hiding of property. And this rationale applies to all of the land of Canaan, and he was caught in their hands and sent spies for this purpose, for there is no evil in the matter. Likewise Joshua sent spies for a necessary reason as I have written, and there is no evil — except specifically in the motive for spying that was in Israel's mind, by stealing the mind of Moses." "<b>And therefore</b> the word of HaShem came to Moses and He said <b>send for yourself</b> — meaning, for what you reckon: the mission that is for the sake of the hiding of property — meaning, but not for the reason that they are thinking. And He alerted him that their thoughts are not His thoughts. And it is possible that Moses did not understand the intent in its precise order, and understood a different way, as I will explain. Or we may say that he did understand, but since he saw that HaShem did not prevent him from sending, he took action and did not act shrewdly against the word of the King. And although HaShem revealed it to him — HaShem desires to reveal to His servants the hidden things, also to know a reason for everything that results from the matter. And if you would raise a difficulty before our God — why did He not prevent the matter from being so? — it is because He saw that without this they would defect more and act more rashly than they did in the sending of the spies." "<b>Further,</b> the statement ״for yourself״ may intend that since He mentioned them in a term of importance and said ״men,״ which indicates that they were righteous, therefore He said ״for yourself״ — meaning, as long as they are before you, with you, they are righteous; but when they turn their faces to go on the mission, they are entirely stripped of it, as in the verse's statement (Numbers 13:26): ״וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ״, and they of blessed memory said (Sotah 35a) that Scripture juxtaposes their going to their coming, etc. From this Moses sensed and prayed over Joshua that he not go in the counsel of the wicked. By this approach the words of our Sages of blessed memory are explained, who said (Numbers Rabbah 16), and this is their language: But the spies whom Moses sent were wicked, as it is written: ״שְׁלַח לְךָ אֲנָשִׁים״ — end quote. And it is not known what they of blessed memory intended: from where do they prove the matter from the statement ״send for yourself״ — that they were wicked? Rather, they analyzed the word <b>for yourself</b> as we have analyzed it — for you they are men, but on the mission, when they begin their journey, they are wicked. One may further say that the meaning of <b>for yourself</b> is: for you they are righteous, but I know that they are wicked. And if you would ask: why would HaShem agree to send wicked men, all the more so since the verse says ״עַל פִּי ה׳״ and they of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah there) that the Holy One, blessed be He, would say: So-and-so for the tribe of So-and-so — perhaps it is because in this particular matter of spying, all Israel were equal in one mind to spy, until they spied; and that was to see whether they could conquer them according to the ability that was in Israel, and this tells of a lack of faith, and therefore they are called wicked." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that, since the intent of those who sent them was evil, the power of evil would be activated in the emissary, and — in accordance with the principle that a man's emissary is like himself — an evil disposition would be born in him that had not been in him before, just as the aspect of holiness is born in the emissaries of a mitzvah for good as a sign. Therefore, even though they were righteous, at the very outset of their journey to spy, an evil disposition was born in them through the power of those who sent them, and they conspired to lead astray." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to say <b>send for yourself</b> — that the mission would be attributed in the name of Moshe and not in His name, may He be blessed, because of what resulted from the matter." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to say to them that the intent of their going should not be what Israel thinks, but rather what Moshe thinks concerning the mission. That is the meaning of <b>send for yourself</b> — according to what is in your mind, and not according to what is in their minds." "<b>Further,</b> He hinted in the word <b>for yourself</b> in accordance with what they said, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 5:23), concerning the verse ״עַתָּה תִרְאֶה״ — ״but in the war against thirty-one kings you will not see,״ end quote. From now on: if Israel had entered the Land in the second year after the Exodus from Egypt, and the statement of HaShem — ״עַתָּה תִרְאֶה״ — had been fulfilled, Moshe would have been compelled to sit outside the Land as an ordinary person, or to enter the Land of Israel as an ordinary person. But through the intermediary of the spies' mission Israel was delayed forty years, and Moshe did not descend from his greatness, and he was king in Jeshurun for forty years until the fullness of his days. That is the meaning of His saying <b>send for yourself</b> — for your benefit is this, in the ultimate outcome of things." "<b>And they shall spy out, etc.</b> It is difficult that it says <b>and they shall spy out</b>, for it should have said ״to spy out״, etc. Further, it is difficult that it says <b>which I am giving</b>, etc., for after He mentioned the Place [i.e., the divine Name] there was no need to say ״which I [am giving]״, etc., for who does not know that He is giving it to them?" "Yet according to what we have explained — that He stipulated in the mission a first condition, ״לְךָ״, meaning, according to whatever he considers that they should be emissaries of Mosheh, along with the rest of the explanations we gave for it — and He further added another condition, ״אֲנָשִׁים״, requiring that they be righteous — He further comes and adds yet another condition in the matter: that the scouting mission is <b>אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נֹתֵן לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל</b>, meaning: not to assess whether that land — do they have the power to conquer it and dispossess its people? For this is certain: they have no ability to stand even against a single small city among them. Rather, <b>אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נֹתֵן</b> — and since that thing comes to them as a gift, it is upon the Giver to remove its oppressors according to the law of one who gives a gift, and He will wage war for them and establish it in their hands with no adversary and no evil encounter. Moreover, they will gain insight through their scouting, that HaShem will perform wonders to humble the exalted — a great and lofty people, many and mighty — so that the beneficiary of the miracle will recognize His miracle. And through this, HaShem in His kindness acted wisely and removed a stumbling block from before them: when they see Amalek etc., and the offspring of Anaq etc., and the formidable strength of the fortified cities, they will not fear and be terrified, for their inheritance is not by Israel's power but by the power of the Creator, Who is capable of all, for He is the Giver." "<b>And this</b> is the statement <b>אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נֹתֵן</b>, and in the sweetness of its expression He revealed that by nature they are no match for them, as the scouts themselves said afterward — and through this the fine points are resolved. However, it remains to investigate this: why was it not sufficient to say ״וְיָתוּרוּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נֹתֵן,״ without needing to mention <b>אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן</b>? And I have seen from our Rabbis of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni) that they sensed this difficulty and said that it comes to allude to the merit of the one who occasioned the gift, namely the merit of Yitsḥaq, who was born to Avraham and Sarah — Avraham one hundred years old and Sarah ninety years old — which, in the combined count of both their years, is one hundred ninety (ק״צ), and this is the way of midrash. But it appears to say that the verse intended to convey the very statement that Mosheh said to Israel in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 9:5): ״לֹא בְצִדְקָתְךָ וגו׳, כִּי בְּרִשְׁעַת הַגּוֹיִם וגו׳״ — and that is what he alluded to here when he said ״אֲנִי נֹתֵן אֶרֶץ <b>כְּנַעַן</b>״, to say: on account of its being the land of Canaan, whose measure has been filled — therefore ״I am giving it.״" "<b>תִּשְׁלָחוּ.</b> And it was not sufficient with the statement ״שְׁלַח״ at the beginning of the verse. Perhaps it comes to stipulate that they should not go except in this arrangement — ״אִישׁ אֶחָד״ etc. — neither more nor less."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :register {:flags {3 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:05.523328325Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}